go-images/jpeg2000

This product includes software developed by AJ Roetker
(https://github.com/ajroetker/go-jpeg2000), licensed under the Apache
License, Version 2.0.

This is a fork. As required by section 4(b) of that licence, the changes
carried here are stated:

  2026-09-21  A four-component picture whose own JP2 header declares the
              enumerated colour space CMYK (EnumCS 12, ITU-T T.800 Table I.1)
              now decodes to an *image.CMYK rather than to an *image.RGBA
              built from its first three components. Reading those four planes
              as red, green and blue discarded the black plate; on the
              reference picture that was 255 levels from poppler on every
              pixel, and it is now at most 2 over 2 440 044 samples. The
              multi-component transform, where a codestream signals one, is
              undone across the first three planes exactly as before; the
              fourth is outside it and is kept. Decode and
              DecodeWithUpsampling take the same decision, so the two entry
              points cannot disagree about what a file is.

              Files: color.go (convertToCMYK, convertToCMYKFloat, sameShape),
              decoder.go (isCMYK, picture, pictureUpsampled, toCMYK),
              cmyk_test.go, go.mod (module path).

  2026-09-29  A tile is given its position in the reference grid, so a
              multi-tile encode round-trips. The encoder anchored a tile's
              code-block grid at the tile's own origin where ITU-T T.800 B.7
              anchors it at the REFERENCE grid's, and it computed subband
              extents from a tile's size rather than from its coordinates
              (B-14, B-15). Measured over sixteen tile grids of a 300x300
              image, reversible: EIGHT of the sixteen did not survive a
              lossless round trip, and none does now. Six of them were read
              back by OpenJPEG's opj_decompress as well, including a grid
              whose last tile is one pixel wide. A one-row resolution is also
              scaled in the forward transform the way the inverse already
              scaled it (Table F.4), and a tile whose coefficients all
              quantise away is written and read rather than refused -- that
              refusal was "no packet data read", and an empty tile is a legal
              tile.

              Files: blockgrid.go (new), dwt_encode.go, encoder.go, packet.go,
              tilegrid_test.go (new), blockgrid_test.go (new).

  2026-09-28  A decode holds far less memory, and a one-component picture
              comes back as one byte a pixel. Measured against the same
              pictures: a grey page went from 19.8 to 8.6 bytes a pixel
              (917 MB peak to 397 MB) and a colour one from 76.4 to 20.2
              (2 416 MB to 638 MB). Four things did it: a component's rows
              come out of one allocation rather than one per row; a
              whole-component tile's coefficients are handed over instead of
              copied; the 9/7 path holds ONE buffer a component where it held
              two, by reinterpreting the int32 plane as float32 in place, the
              way OpenJPEG's tcd.c does; and the inverse ICT is held a band of
              rows at a time rather than a page. The 9/7 coefficients are also
              carried at the precision the reference carries them.

              Files: view.go (new), decoder.go, dwt.go, color.go, tile.go,
              view_test.go (new).

  2026-09-26  A codestream whose component origin is odd no longer crashes the
              9/7 synthesis, and the vertical pass takes eight columns at a
              time through go-highway. Behaviour is bit-for-bit as before
              where it did not crash.

              Files: dwt.go, color.go, ebcot.go.

  Also: the tests now run on macOS, Windows, 386, arm, ppc64le, riscv64,
  s390x and loong64, which upstream only ever cross-compiled; the 386 lane
  runs natively because qemu-i386 loses float state. No behaviour of the
  library is changed by that, and it is stated because it is why several of
  the fixes above were found.
